A||||18||JohnF||Opening decklid without key?||||8||
Z||000000||JohnF||10-11-2001||06:20 PM||||O.K.  the dumba$$ act & now question of the day.  <P>With a Fiero I have had for about 3 hours, I just disconnected the battery and *&%^%#^ shut the power engine decklid. There was no key available for the decklid!<P>Without using a beer can opener, cutting torch or other destructive device (dynamite comes to mind!!!!!), how do I open the deck lid?||||reg||8
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-11-2001||06:27 PM||||no key?  sorry to say the easiest would be drilling out the lock and getting an replacement (with key) at the wreckers for  $1.00 <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||WampusCat||10-11-2001||06:42 PM||||You could try removing the fuse for the trunklid release and plugging a 12vdc powersource into that slot long enough to power the decklid release.  Got a laptop ac to dc converter?  Plug the + into the fuse slot and the - to a groud somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000003||jdrais||10-11-2001||06:57 PM||||I ran into a similar problem once.  (Battery dead, cylinder was junk).<P>What I ended up doing was unscrewing the switch from the dash and then took a 12V battery and touched the two wires on the back of the switch.  Popped right open. <P>Jeff Drais<p>[This message has been edited by jdrais (edited 10-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-11-2001||07:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jdrais:<BR><B>I ran into a similar problem once.  (Battery dead, cylinder was junk).<P>What I ended up doing was unscrewing the switch from the dash and then took a 12V battery and touched the two wires on the back of the switch.  Popped right open. <P>Jeff Drais<P>The voice of experience sounds excellent!<P>[This message has been edited by jdrais (edited 10-11-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake_Dragon||10-11-2001||07:39 PM||||That's why I have a knob instead of a lock.<BR>Don't keep anything valuable in there.<BR>Dropped the keys in the trunk the first week I had it and could not get it open for nothing, punched out the lock with a large screw driver.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Jake_Dragon (edited 10-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||SLYGUY||10-12-2001||12:04 AM||||Jake...<P>There is something valuable in there. The ENGINE!!<P>LOL<BR>Glad you found a way around it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||10-12-2001||12:07 AM||||I had to power up through the cigarette lighter a couple times when my battery went dead.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pike||10-12-2001||01:38 AM||||Easy!...just apply 12 vdc across the fuse and push the button!!!   :&gt; )<P>Huh?<P>Pike||||reg||1
Z||000009||onfire||10-12-2001||03:51 AM||||Yeah but what happens when the solenoid goes out also? Or what if you don't have a power trunk?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/onfiresigtoo.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnF||10-12-2001||07:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>I had to power up through the cigarette lighter a couple times when my battery went dead.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This sucker isn't DEAD - it is disconnected!!!!!!  Thanks for the idea, though.<P>||||reg||9
Z||000011||Master_Sushi||10-12-2001||08:57 AM||||I'll be screwed if this happens to me. I removed and then filled in the decklid key hole. Now all I have to open the decklid is the decklid pop. I'd like to know what else to do to get in!<P>Im thinking maybe somehow remove the engine vents to the back side of the decklid. Then unbolt and remove the whole decklid. Seems like a PITA though. I cant think of anything else.||||reg||11
Z||000012||Indiana_resto_guy||10-12-2001||09:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Master_Sushi:<BR><B>I'll be screwed if this happens to me. I removed and then filled in the decklid key hole. Now all I have to open the decklid is the decklid pop. I'd like to know what else to do to get in!<P>Im thinking maybe somehow remove the engine vents to the back side of the decklid. Then unbolt and remove the whole decklid. Seems like a PITA though. I cant think of anything else.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You might look into rigging a cable pull release like up front.||||reg||1
Z||000013||gomobile||10-12-2001||10:38 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B> You might look into rigging a cable pull release like up front.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have to put in my 2 cents. Where was the key for the trunk-lid? I say cause I would never put myself in a situation where I would be dependent on SWITCHES only (its like having a door without a locking mechanism). What happens if you lost power? And yes I agree you do have something in the back the ENGINE especially if you have modify it to run better and faster.||||reg||6
Z||000014||Master_Sushi||10-12-2001||11:54 AM||||I think I will try to rig up a cable incase the trunk pop decides not to work. Only reason I got rid of the trunk lock is because it was allready drilled out, and im to cheap to get a new one. My decklid is shot anyway. If I really had to get in id just use a BFH to break into it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 10-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnF||10-12-2001||01:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gomobile:<BR><B> Where was the key for the trunk-lid? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't look the gift-horse in the mouth.  When a friend of mine said "take the car, it's yours for free!" and gave me only an ignition key, I didn't ask too many questions as I hurriedly drove it off.  <P>BTW- Pontiac can still (with proof of ownership) supply keys for these cars with  per the VIN.  While the title is being transferred, that is a problem.<P>||||reg||12
Z||000016||gomobile||10-12-2001||01:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B> I don't look the gift-horse in the mouth.  When a friend of mine said "take the car, it's yours for free!" and gave me only an ignition key, I didn't ask too many questions as I hurriedly drove it off.  <P>BTW- Pontiac can still (with proof of ownership) supply keys for these cars with  per the VIN.  While the title is being transferred, that is a problem.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I hear you and too bad that you went through some hassles as NO one likes to pop the trunk(lid). Especially if the battery is DEAD and there is no other remedy to get to it.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000017||JohnF||10-12-2001||04:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jdrais:<BR><B>I ran into a similar problem once.  <BR>What I ended up doing was unscrewing the switch from the dash and then took a 12V battery and touched the two wires on the back of the switch.  Popped right open. <P>Jeff Drais<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I always try the easiest first and it was an absolutely perfect reply!  IT WORKED!  A 12V battery charger with leads touching the bottom two connectors of the dash wired connector and WHAMMO! an open deck lid!<P>Thanks to all who replied and especially to Jeff Drais.<P>||||reg||6
